**Read:** [Geological Structures: a Practical Introduction — Waldron and Snyder](../sources/Geological-Structures-Waldron-Snyder.lingua#r_2RC4KMK6ADFX90708NZH2F6RRW), the full Orientation section and Lab 1 Orientation of Lines and Planes; [Digital Cartographic Standard for Geologic Map Symbolization — FGDC/USGS](../sources/Geologic-Map-Symbolization.lingua#r_7DHXSTN5K3KF1EPTPG18RM5K1K), the complete orientation-symbol conventions used; [Introduction to Geology — Perron and Jagoutz, MIT 12.001](../sources/MIT-Introduction-to-Geology.lingua#r_423FFVY46CGEN2F2263F95KX6N), orientation/stereonet preparation.

A plane's strike is the direction of its horizontal line; dip gives its downward inclination and direction. A line uses trend and plunge. These describe different geometrical objects: a lineation lying on bedding is not specified by bedding's orientation alone. State azimuth range, north reference, declination treatment, and the strike/dip convention.

Under a right-hand strike convention the dip direction is 90° clockwise from strike. A plane dipping east at 30° therefore has right-hand strike 000° and dip 30° toward 090°. An unoriented strike line also runs south; confusing the two equivalent line directions with an assigned dip direction creates a different plane.

Compass clinometer measurements require an identified surface/line, suitable instrument procedure, and checks for local interference and accessibility. Separate instrument reading uncertainty from variability in a curved, rough, folded, or poorly exposed feature. A reported average must identify the population and appropriate directional method.

**Work:** complete orientation Lab 1. Translate several equivalent plane notations into sketches and a consistent notation, including horizontal and vertical cases. Measure suitable known surfaces or models, repeat independently, and compare with a geometric construction. Record the instrument/reference conditions and distinguish repeatability from actual spatial variation.

Read the full orientation sections before the map and stereonet problems. Coordinate conventions will remain explicit in the original three-point exercise, which handles a height-representable plane but cannot represent a vertical one.

**Continue:** [Interpret fold geometry, stratigraphic facing, and overprinting together](Folds-facing-and-overprinting.lingua#r_4YNWT9JHWFDN11HKFQW6XJN998).

